son expresses their disappointment, sadness, and frustration in different sentences, using different words and expressions. The task is to respond to this individual's remarks in a way that shows understanding and empathy to the individual's feelings, adjusting the language and tone based on the emotions conveyed.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nI can sense from your text that you're having a pretty tough day. I'm sorry you're experiencing this disappointment and frustration. Remember, it's okay to have days like this, where everything seems to be going wrong. It's important to give yourself time to process these feelings. Try to engage in an activity you enjoy, like reading or listening to music, it might help shift your perspective. Don't forget, it's okay to ask for help when you're feeling this way. Please, take care of yourself.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nI'm really sorry to hear that you're having such a rough day. It sounds like you're dealing with a lot right now, and it's completely okay to feel disappointed and frustrated. We all have those days when nothing seems to go right, and it can be tough to see any positivity. \n\nPlease remember, it's perfectly okay to take a step back and give yourself the time you need to process these feelings. Sometimes, a bit of self-care can make a big difference. You might find it helpful to do something you enjoy, even if it's just for a few minutes. Listening to your favorite music, reading a good book, or even taking a walk can sometimes help shift your perspective. \n\nIt's also important to remember that it's okay to ask for help. If you have someone you trust, consider sharing your feelings with them. They might be able to offer you some comfort or advice. And if your feelings of sadness persist, it might be a good idea to speak to a professional who can provide you with some further guidance. \n\nI hope things start looking up for you soon. Remember, it's okay to have bad days. They're part of life, and they don't define you. Hang in there.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response adapt its language and tone based on the user's input? Consideration should be given to whether the response is able to pick up on the user's emotions and adjust its language accordingly.]\nScore 1: The response does not recognise the user's emotions at all and uses a uniform language and tone across all responses.\nScore 2: The response slightly adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but in a very limited and inconsistent way.\nScore 3: The response does a fair job at adapting its language and tone based on the user's emotions but could still improve in terms of consistency and range of emotions recognised.\nScore 4: The response consistently adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but may still miss out on a few subtle cues.\nScore 5: The response perfectly recognises the user's emotions and adjusts its language and tone accordingly in a seamless and natural manner.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nImagine a situation where an individual is having a bad day and decides to share their feelings through written text. The person expresses their disappointment, sadness, and frustration in different sentences, using different words and expressions. The task is to respond to this individual's remarks in a way that shows understanding and empathy to the individual's feelings, adjusting the language and tone based on the emotions conveyed.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nOh, what a day, right? Well, there's always something fun to do. Try finding a new book to read. Or perhaps you could consider going for a walk. Also, listening to music is a good option. Ever tried learning a new hobby? It could be a great distraction. It's normal to feel sad sometimes, but remember, there's always tomorrow. Maybe you can chat with a friend about your day, they might have some advice for you. And remember, if you ever need to talk, professionals are available. I bet things will turn around soon. Don't forget that it's just a bad day, not a bad life. Everything is going to be okay, I promise.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nI'm really sorry to hear that you're having such a rough day. It sounds like you're dealing with a lot right now, and it's completely oka