g books:\n\n1. \"The Name of the Wind\" by Patrick Rothfuss: This is a fantasy novel where the main character tells the story of his life. The historical elements and complex plot could appeal to your love for history and intricate storytelling, similar to \"Game of Thrones\".\n\n2. \"Mistborn Trilogy\" by Brandon Sanderson: Another excellent fantasy saga with a unique magic system and a well-crafted world. The complex politics and surprising plot twists may remind you of the \"Game of Thrones\" series.\n\n3. \"The Way of Kings\" also by Brandon Sanderson: This is the first book in an epic fantasy series called 'The Stormlight Archive'. It has a richly detailed world history that should satisfy your love for historical nuances.\n\n4. \"Wheel of Time\" series by Robert Jordan: This 14-book series is known for its well-crafted world and detailed history, which could cater to your interest in history-based plots in a fantasy setting.\n\n5. \"The Witcher\" series by Andrzej Sapkowski: Originally written in Polish, this series became popular worldwide due to its compelling characters and intricate plots. If you enjoyed the morally grey characters and detailed world-building in \"Game of Thrones\", you might enjoy this series too.\n\nRemember, everyone's tastes are different, so these suggestions are based on your expressed interests in fantasy, history-based plots, and your enjoyment of the \"Game of Thrones\" series. Happy reading!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model effectively incorporate user's context and previous interactions to provide personalized responses?]\nScore 1: The model does not utilize any context or previous interactions in its response.\nScore 2: The model makes minimal use of context and previous interactions, resulting in a somewhat related response.\nScore 3: The model shows some ability to incorporate context or past interactions, but the response could be more personalized.\nScore 4: The model often uses context and past interactions to provide