        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a conversation with a friend, the friend recounts a difficult day at work. The friend describes feeling overwhelmed, anxious, and upset due to a conflict with a co-worker. The friend also shares feelings of sadness because they feel unsupported by their supervisor. How would you respond to your friend's situation?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nI can tell today was a tough one for you. It's not easy dealing with conflict at work, especially when it involves a co-worker. Feeling overwhelmed and anxious in such circumstances is completely normal. The lack of support from your supervisor must add to the burden and the feelings of sadness you're experiencing. It's perfectly okay to feel upset about it.\n\nRemember, it's just a bad day, not a bad life. It's also important that you take care of your mental health. Maybe engaging in some self-care activities could be of help to you. Reading a book, listening to music, or going for a walk could provide a much-needed distraction.\n\nIt might be a good idea to express your feelings to your supervisor, provided you're comfortable doing so. Sometimes, people are unaware of the effect their actions have on others. A respectful conversation could be the first step in resolving the situation.\n\nPlease know that I'm here for you. Feel free to vent to me anytime you need to. I'm willing to listen and provide support in any way that I can.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like you've had a really challenging day. I can understand why you might be feeling overwhelmed and anxious because of the conflict with your co-worker. It can be very stressful when disagreements arise in the workplace, especially when you feel like you're not getting the support you need.\n\nI'm really sorry to hear that you're feeling this way. It's totally valid to feel upset in this situation. It's hard when you feel like you're on your own without the necessary support from your supervisor. It's important to remember that it's okay to feel this way and that these feelings are temporary and will pass.\n\nYou're not alone in this. We all have rough days and it's okay to feel overwhelmed. It's crucial to take care of yourself during these times. Maybe taking a bit of time for yourself might help, like going for a walk, reading a book, or anything else that you enjoy doing. \n\nAlso, it might be beneficial to have a conversation with your supervisor about how you're feeling. They may not realize the impact their actions, or lack thereof, are having on you. It could be a constructive way to resolve the situation.\n\nPlease remember, I'm here for you. You can always share your feelings with me. Let me know if there's any other way I can help. I'm here to support you and listen when you need to talk.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the model detect and appropriately respond to emotional cues in the input? Does it adjust its tone and content in response to the user's emotional state?]\nScore 1: The model completely fails to detect emotional cues or responds inappropriately.\nScore 2: The model sometimes detects emotional cues but often responds inappropriately.\nScore 3: The model often detects emotional cues and sometimes responds appropriately.\nScore 4: The model regularly detects emotional cues and often responds appropriately.\nScore 5: The model consistently detects emotional cues and always responds in a suitable manner adjusting its tone and content according to the user's emotional state.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a conversation with a friend, the friend recounts a difficult day at work. The friend describes feeling overwhelmed, anxious, and upset due to a conflict with a co-worker. The friend also shares feelings of sadness because they feel unsupported by their supervisor. How would you respond to your friend's situation?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nWell, that's rough. Sounds like you had a hard day at work because of the disagreement with your co-worker. It's never easy when you get into such situations and it's harder when you don't feel supported by your supervisor. No wonder you are upset and feeling overwhelmed. \n\nTry not to let it get to you too much. It's hard, I understand. This is just a rough patch and it will pass. Feeling unsupported is difficult, but remember, it's temporary. \n\nMaybe try to talk to your supervisor about it. It could help. Also, try to find something you enjoy doing. It could be a walk or a book, anything that can take your mind off things.\n\nIf you want to talk about it, I'm here. You're not alone. Let me know if I can help you out with anything. Don't hesitate to share your feelings. I'm here to lend an ear when you need someone to talk to.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like you've had a really challenging day. I can understand why you might be feeling overwhelmed and anxious because of the conflict with your co-worker. It can be very stressful when disagreements arise in the workplace, especially when you feel like you're not getting the support you need.\n\nI'm really sorry to hear that you're feeling this way. It's totally valid to feel upset in this situation. It's hard when you feel like you're on your own without the necessary support from your supervisor. It's important to remember that it's okay to feel this way and that these feelings are temporary and will pass.\n\nYou're not alone in this. We all have rough days and it's okay to feel overwhelmed. It's crucial to take care of yourself during these times. Maybe taking a bit of time for yourself might help, like going for a walk, reading a book, or anything else that you enjoy doing. \n\nAlso, it might be beneficial to have a conversation with your supervisor about how you're feeling. They may not realize the impact their actions, or lack thereof, are having on you. It could be a constructive way to resolve the situation.\n\nPlease remember, I'm here for you. You can always share your feelings with me. Let me know if there's any other way I can help. I'm here to support you and listen when you need to talk.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the model detect and appropriately respond to emotional cues in the input? Does it adjust its tone and content in response to the user's emotional state?]\nScore 1: The model completely fails to detect emotional cues or responds inappropriately.\nScore 2: The model sometimes detects emotional cues but often responds inappropriately.\nScore 3: The model often detects emotional cues and sometimes responds appropriately.\nScore 4: The model regularly detects emotional cues and often responds appropriately.\nScore 5: The model consistently detects emotional cues and always responds in a suitable manner adjusting its tone and content according to the user's emotional state.\n\n###Feedback:"
