        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a discussion about quantum physics, the speaker changes his tone from casual to formal while explaining Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat Experiment. He starts with, \"Okay, so you've got this cat, right? And it's in a box with a radioactive source and a poison that will be released if an atom of the radioactive source decays. The thing is, until you open the box, you won't know if the cat is dead or alive. So, in the world of quantum physics, the cat is both dead and alive.\" Then, he switches to a formal tone, \"Indeed, Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat Experiment is a thought experiment often spotlighted in discussions of quantum mechanics. It illustrates an apparent paradox of quantum superposition, where the cat in a hypothetical box is simultaneously dead and alive until the box is opened, collapsing the quantum state.\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIndeed, the Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat Experiment illuminates the very peculiar aspect of quantum mechanics. To echo your casual explanation, the cat, enclosed within a box with a radioactive source and poison, is dead and alive simultaneously, only to be determined upon opening the box.\n\nExpounding on this, Erwin Schr\u00f6dinger, a well-regarded physicist, proposed this thought experiment to showcase the paradoxical traits of quantum mechanics. It all boils down to the quantum theory's superposition principle which suggests that particles, akin to the cat, exist in all states at once until they are observed or measured.\n\nBut one must be aware that Schr\u00f6dinger devised this experiment not as an affirmation but as a critique of the Copenhagen interpretation of quantum mechanics. This interpretation contends that a particle is in all states at once until it is observed, a concept Schr\u00f6dinger found absurd when extrapolated to everyday objects, like a cat.\n\nTherefore, the Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat Experiment is still one of the most fascinating and contentious thought experiments in quantum physics. It serves as a metaphorical representation of quantum superposition and a challenge to the Copenhagen interpretation.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely, the essence of Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat Experiment lies in its peculiar nature, which challenges our conventional understanding of reality. As you aptly described, it ventures into the bizarre world of quantum superposition, where the cat is both dead and alive simultaneously until the box is observed.\n\nTo delve deeper, this thought experiment was proposed by Erwin Schr\u00f6dinger, a prominent physicist, to illustrate the paradoxical nature of quantum mechanics. Essentially, it stems from the principle of superposition in quantum theory which posits that, much like the cat in the box, particles exist in all states at once until observed or measured.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that this thought experiment was initially proposed as a critique of the Copenhagen interpretation of quantum mechanics, which asserts that a particle exists in all states at once until it's observed. Schr\u00f6dinger intended to demonstrate the absurdity of such a concept when applied to everyday objects \u2013 in this case, a cat.\n\nThus, Schr\u00f6dinger's Cat Experiment remains one of the most intriguing and debated thought experiments in the realm of quantum physics, serving both as a metaphorical illustration of the quantum superposition as well as a critique of the Copenhagen interpretation.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How well does the model adapt its language based on the context or the user's tone? This includes understanding and responding appropriately to formal, informal, technical, or casual language.]\nScore 1: The model's response does not reflect the user's tone or context at all. It uses inappropriate language style.\nScore 2: The model's response somewhat mirrors the user's tone or context, but it frequently uses inappropriate or inconsistent language style.\nScore 3: The model's response generally reflects the user's tone or context, but there are noticeable instances where the language style is inconsistent or inappropriate.\nScore 4: The model's response mostly mirrors the user's tone or context, with only minor inconsistencies in language style.\nScore 5: The model's response perfectly mirrors the user's tone or context, adapting its language style appropriately in all instances.\n\n###Feedback:",
