        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a historical discussion forum, a post asks about the contextual meaning behind Shakespeare's famous quote, \"All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players.\" The post seeks to understand the quote's implication on the human condition as seen from the Elizabethan era's perspective, and how this can be related to the modern world context.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nShakespeare's well-known line, \"All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players,\" hails from his play \"As You Like It.\" It's a comparison of life to a play where we're all actors, each performing in different scenes of life, which was a reflection of the Elizabethan era's outlook on existence. Back then, the society was pretty hierarchical, meaning folks were kinda stuck playing the part they were born into, much like characters in a script.\n\nBut fast forward to today, and the stage has changed quite a bit. We're not so much bound by our birth anymore, and we get to pick and choose our roles more freely. Sure, we still have some societal norms and expectations to grapple with, but it's not as rigid. That being said, the quote still holds true. It's almost like a reminder that we're all just playing parts in the grand play that is life, whether it's a role we're born into or one we've picked for ourselves.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe quote \"All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players\" comes from Shakespeare's play \"As You Like It\" and reflects the playwright's insightful commentary on human life and behavior. This metaphor of life as a theater, where each person plays their part in seven distinct \"acts\" or stages, is a profound expression of the human condition as perceived during the Elizabethan era.\n\nIn this period, the societal structure was quite stratified, and people were expected to play their roles according to their social standing, much like actors on a stage. This belief influenced behavior, relationships, and the overall conduct of life. People were born into their 'roles' and 'stages,' and they had little opportunity to deviate from them.\n\nIn the modern context, this quote may still resonate but in a significantly different way. The rigid societal structures of the Elizabethan era have largely been replaced by more fluid and flexible ones. However, the concept of people playing 'roles' in their lives remains. Today, these roles might be influenced by personal ambition, societal expectations, or cultural norms, among other factors. \n\nThe quote, therefore, can be interpreted as an enduring commentary on the human condition. It reflects the universality and timelessness of the human experience, encapsulating the notion that we all are actors on the stage of life, playing our parts according to the 'scripts' we have been given or have chosen for ourselves.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model use appropriate language and tone depending on the context of the conversation? Can it adapt its responses to align with the nature of the conversation, including professional, casual, formal, or informal contexts?]\nScore 1: The model's language and tone are inappropriate for the context of the conversation, showing no adaptability.\nScore 2: The model's language and tone are somewhat appropriate, with occasional misalignments with the conversation's context.\nScore 3: The model's language and tone are generally appropriate for the context, but there are instances where it does not adapt well to the conversation's nature.\nScore 4: The model's language and tone are mostly appropriate for the context, with minor instances of misalignment.\nScore 5: The model's language and tone perfectly align with the conversation's context, demonstrating strong adaptability to the nature of the conversation.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a historical discussion forum, a post asks about the contextual meaning behind Shakespeare's famous quote, \"All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players.\" The post seeks to understand the quote's implication on the human condition as seen from the Elizabethan era's perspective, and how this can be related to the modern world context.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nSo, the quote \"All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players\" is like, you know, all about life and stuff. It's from Shakespeare, and he was like, big on drama and all. So, back in those days, people had roles and stuff. Like, if you were born a prince, you had to act like a prince, you know? It's like everyone's life was a play, and they were acting or something.\n\nAnd, yeah, things are different now. We've got more choices, and we don't have to play the same roles our parents did. But we've still got roles, you know? Like, you've got to act a certain way at work, or with your friends, or whatever. So, it's kinda like we're still actors, but the stage is different, and the script's changed.\n\nI guess what I'm saying is, Shakespeare's quote still makes sense today, just in a different way. We're still on a stage, playing our parts, 