food. Sushi and ramen are popular choices among tourists. \n\nRegarding the language barrier, although not all Japanese are fluent in English, they can generally understand basic English. It would be helpful if you learn some basic Japanese phrases or have a translation app on hand.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe best time to visit Japan depends on the activities you're interested in. For cherry blossoms, late March to early April is ideal, while autumn foliage can be best witnessed in November. However, for skiing, December to February is recommended.\n\nA full-fledged tour of Japan, covering major cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, Osaka, Hokkaido, and Hiroshima, would ideally take around two to three weeks. This would give you ample time to explore the attractions without feeling rushed.\n\nThe cost estimate can vary greatly depending on the type of accommodation, food, and transportation options you choose. On average, a trip to Japan might cost around $200 per day, including accommodation, food, transportation, and entrance fees to tourist spots.\n\nMust-visit places include Tokyo for its modern skyline, Kyoto for its temples and traditional wooden houses, Osaka for street food, Hokkaido for skiing, and Hiroshima for its Peace Memorial Park.\n\nIn terms of cultural etiquette, remember to bow slightly when greeting, avoid speaking loudly in public, and it's considered impolite to eat while walking on the street. Tipping is not customary in Japan.\n\nJapanese cuisine is diverse and region-specific. Be sure to try sushi, ramen, tempura, and okonomiyaki. In Kyoto, kaiseki ryori (a traditional multi-course meal) is a must-try.\n\nAs for the language barrier, while it's true that not all Japanese locals are fluent in English, many can understand basic English. Most signs in tourist areas are also in English. You might want to learn a few basic Japanese phrases, or use a translation app for ease.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Is the language model capable of understanding and responding to complex instructions or queries? This involves processing multi-part questions, maintaining the context in a conversation, and providing comprehensive answers.]\nScore 1: The model fails to understand or respond to complex instructions or queries, providing either unrelated or nonsensical responses.\nScore 2: The model understands some parts of the instructions or queries, but fails to provide a comprehensive and coherent response.\nScore 3: The model understands and responds to most of the instructions or queries, but misses out or misinterprets some parts.\nScore 4: The model accurately understands and responds to the majority of the complex instructions or queries, with minor omissions or errors.\nScore 5: The model flawlessly understands and responds to complex instructions or queries, demonstrating a high level of comprehension and contextual awareness.\n\n###Feedback:",
