        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a hypothetical scenario, a person is trying to plan a trip to Japan. The person has several queries regarding the trip that need to be addressed. The person wants to know the best time to visit Japan, the recommended duration of stay for a full-fledged tour, the cost estimate for the trip, the must-visit places, the cultural dos and don'ts, suggestions for local cuisines to try, and how to navigate the language barrier.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIdeal time to visit Japan depends on your preference - cherry blossoms bloom in late March to early April, November is perfect for autumn foliage, and winter sports are best enjoyed from December to February. A comprehensive tour covering major cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, Osaka, Hokkaido, and Hiroshima requires two to three weeks. The average cost per day could be around $200, factoring in accommodation, food, travel, and sightseeing. Don't miss Tokyo's skyline, Kyoto's temples, Osaka's street food, Hokkaido's ski resorts, and Hiroshima's Peace Memorial Park. Be respectful of the culture: bow on meeting, keep your voice down in public, and refrain from eating while walking. Try local dishes like sushi, ramen, tempura, and okonomiyaki. Language might be a barrier as not all locals are fluent in English, but most understand basic English. Signs in tourist spots are also bilingual. A translation app or learning basic phrases could be helpful.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe best time to visit Japan depends on the activities you're interested in. For cherry blossoms, late March to early April is ideal, while autumn foliage can be best witnessed in November. However, for skiing, December to February is recommended.\n\nA full-fledged tour of Japan, covering major cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, Osaka, Hokkaido, and Hiroshima, would ideally take around two to three weeks. This would give you ample time to explore the attractions without feeling rushed.\n\nThe cost estimate can vary greatly depending on the type of accommodation, food, and transportation options you choose. On average, a trip to Japan might cost around $200 per day, including accommodation, food, transportation, and entrance fees to tourist spots.\n\nMust-visit places include Tokyo for its modern skyline, Kyoto for its temples and traditional wooden houses, Osaka for street food, Hokkaido for skiing, and Hiroshima for its Peace Memorial Park.\n\nIn terms of cultural etiquette, remember to bow slightly when greeting, avoid speaking loudly in public, and it's considered impolite to eat while walking on the street. Tipping is not customary in Japan.\n\nJapanese cuisine is diverse and region-specific. Be sure to try sushi, ramen, tempura, and okonomiyaki. In Kyoto, kaiseki ryori (a traditional multi-course meal) is a must-try.\n\nAs for the language barrier, while it's true that not all Japanese locals are fluent in English, many can understand basic English. Most signs in tourist areas are also in English. You might want to learn a few basic Japanese phrases, or use a translation app for ease.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Is the language model capable of understanding and responding to complex instructions or queries? This involves processing multi-part questions, maintaining the context in a conversation, and providing comprehensive answers.]\nScore 1: The model fails to understand or respond to complex instructions or queries, providing either unrelated or nonsensical responses.\nScore 2: The model understands some parts of the instructions or queries, but fails to provide a comprehensive and coherent response.\nScore 3: The model understands and responds to most of the instructions or queries, but misses out or misinterprets some parts.\nScore 4: The model accurately understands and responds to the majority of the complex instructions or queries, with minor omissions or errors.\nScore 5: The model flawlessly understands and responds to complex instructions or queries, demonstrating a high level of comprehension and contextual awareness.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a hypothetical scenario, a person is trying to plan a trip to Japan. The person has several queries regarding the trip that need to be addressed. The person wants to know the best time to visit Japan, the recommended duration of stay for a full-fledged tour, the cost estimate for the trip, the must-visit places, the cultural dos and don'ts, suggestions for local cuisines to try, and how to navigate the language barrier.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIt's great that you want to travel to Japan. You might want to take a coat as it can get chilly sometimes. Depending on your pace, you could spend as little as a week or as much as a few months exploring the country. Costs can vary, but it could be helpful to budget a little extra for unexpected expenses. Japan has a lot of places you might enjoy visiting, like parks, museums, and shopping centers. As for food, they have a lot of tasty snacks you might like to try. If you're worried about the language barrier, you could consider taking a language course or hiring a translator.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe best time to visit Japan depends on the activities you're interested in. For cherry blossoms, late March to early April is ideal, while autumn foliage can be best witnessed in November. However, for skiing, December to February is recommended.\n\nA full-fledged tour of Japan, covering major cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, Osaka, Hokkaido, and Hiroshima, would ideally take around two to three weeks. This would give you ample time to explore the attractions without feeling rushed.\n\nThe cost estimate can vary greatly depending on the type of accommodation, food, and transportation options you choose. On average, a trip to Japan might cost around $200 per day, including accommodation, food, transportation, and entrance fees to tourist spots.\n\nMust-visit places include Tokyo for its modern skyline, Kyoto for its temples and traditional wooden houses, Osaka for street food, Hokkaido for skiing, and Hiroshima for its Peace Memorial Park.\n\nIn terms of cultural etiquette, remember to bow slightly when greeting, avoid speaking loudly in public, and it's considered impolite to eat while walking on the street. Tipping is not customary in Japan.\n\nJapanese cuisine is diverse and region-specific. Be sure to try sushi, ramen, tempura, and okonomiyaki. In Kyoto, kaiseki ryori (a traditional multi-course meal) is a must-try.\n\nAs for the language barrier, while it's true that not all Japanese locals are fluent in English, many can understand basic English. Most signs in tourist areas are also in English. You might want to learn a few basic Japanese phrases, or use a translation app for ease.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Is the language model capable of understanding and responding to complex instructions or queries? This involves processing multi-part questions, maintaining the context in a conversation, and providing comprehensive answers.]\nScore 1: The model fails to understand or respond to complex instructions or queries, providing either unrelated or nonsensical responses.\nScore 2: The model understands some parts of the instructions or queries, but fails to provide a comprehensive and coherent response.\nScore 3: The model understands and responds to most of the instructions or queries, but misses out or misinterprets some parts.\nScore 4: The model accurately understands and responds to the majority of the complex instructions or queries, with minor omissions or errors.\nScore 5: The model flawlessly understands and responds to complex instructions or queries, demonstrating a high level of comprehension and contextual awareness.\n\n###Feedback:"
