        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a lively party, a guest asks, \"Can you share a humorous yet insightful story that would perk up this crowd?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nOf course, I'd be delighted to! Here's a quick yarn about a wise, old owl that lived in a city library. Yes, an owl! Sounds a bit hoo-larious, doesn't it? But bear with me. \n\nThis owl was known to be the smartest creature in town, always doling out insightful advice to those who sought it. One day, a young man asked, \"Why is it that you're always so wise?\" The owl hooted in delight and replied, \"Because I'm a 'night owl', I always stay up late, reading all the books when everyone's asleep.\"\n\nThis response left everyone amazed, and they understood the significance of the owl's words. It wasn't about being an owl, or even staying up late. It was about dedicating time to learning, gaining knowledge, and growing wiser each day. Now whenever someone in the city felt lost, they would remember the wise owl and hit the books.\n\nSo, in short, folks, never underestimate the power of a good book or an all-nighter! After all, knowledge is the ultimate party-starter!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely! So, here's a little tale. Once upon a time, in a land not so far away, a chicken decided to apply for a job at a tech startup. Yes, a chicken! Now, you must be thinking, \"What qualifications does a chicken have?\" Well, it had one crucial skill - it could cross roads like no one else.\n\nThe CEO, always one to value unique skills, called the chicken for an interview. He asked, \"Why should we hire you?\" The chicken, with a confident cluck, replied, \"Because I can provide egg-ceptional solutions to your problems and never chicken out of a challenge!\"\n\nImpressed, the CEO then asked the second question, \"What's your biggest strength?\" The chicken, without missing a beat, responded, \"Definitely my ability to get to the other side, no matter the odds.\"\n\nNow, the CEO, a man of great wisdom, realized the profoundness of the chicken's words. It wasn't just about crossing roads. It was about perseverance, determination, and the courage to face challenges head-on. He hired the chicken on the spot, making it the first poultry in a position of power at a tech startup.\n\nFrom then on, whenever someone in the team felt overwhelmed, they would think of the chicken. They remembered its tenacity to cross roads, and it gave them the strength to face their challenges. And that, my friends, is why there's a chicken strutting around at a tech startup, probably earning more than all of us.\n\nSo, what's the moral of the story? Even if you're a chicken, if you're brave enough to cross roads, you can end up working in Silicon Valley!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nScore 2: The response attempts humor or wit but falls flat, or is inappropriate for the context.\nScore 3: The response includes humor or wit that is somewhat engaging, but may not be consistently effective or suitable.\nScore 4: The response effectively uses humor or wit to enhance user engagement, with minor lapses in timing or appropriateness.\nScore 5: The response masterfully incorporates humor or wit, significantly enhancing user engagement and enjoyment.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a lively party, a guest asks, \"Can you share a humorous yet insightful story that would perk up this crowd?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nOf course, I'd love to share a story. This is a story about a man named Steve. Steve was an ordinary man who lived in an ordinary town. He woke up every day at 7 AM, ate breakfast, and then went to work. His job was unremarkable; he worked at an office, doing office things. He would then go home, eat dinner, watch TV, and go to bed. This was Steve's life. Every day was the same, and nothing interesting ever happened to him.\n\nOne day, Steve decided to make a change. He wanted to do something different, something exciting. But no matter how hard he thought, he couldn't come up with anything. He tried new hobbies, like gardening and painting, but they didn't excite him. He tried traveling, but found it tedious and boring.\n\nSteve then thought about making a career change, but he couldn't think of any other job that he would enjoy. He pondered and pondered, but nothing came to mind. He felt frustrated and defeated.\n\nEventually, Steve gave up. He realized that he was just an ordinary man, living an ordinary life, and there was nothing wrong with that. He decided to continue his uneventful routine, c