eryone's hearts.\n\nSo, folks, the moral of the story is, it's not about how fast you go, but about never stopping, no matter what. Just like our little snail friend in the race!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely! So, here's a little tale. Once upon a time, in a land not so far away, a chicken decided to apply for a job at a tech startup. Yes, a chicken! Now, you must be thinking, \"What qualifications does a chicken have?\" Well, it had one crucial skill - it could cross roads like no one else.\n\nThe CEO, always one to value unique skills, called the chicken for an interview. He asked, \"Why should we hire you?\" The chicken, with a confident cluck, replied, \"Because I can provide egg-ceptional solutions to your problems and never chicken out of a challenge!\"\n\nImpressed, the CEO then asked the second question, \"What's your biggest strength?\" The chicken, without missing a beat, responded, \"Definitely my ability to get to the other side, no matter the odds.\"\n\nNow, the CEO, a man of great wisdom, realized the profoundness of the chicken's words. It wasn't just about crossing roads. It was about perseverance, determination, and the courage to face challenges head-on. He hired the chicken on the spot, making it the first poultry in a position of power at a tech startup.\n\nFrom then on, whenever someone in the team felt overwhelmed, they would think of the chicken. They remembered its tenacity to cross roads, and it gave them the strength to face their challenges. And that, my friends, is why there's a chicken strutting around at a tech startup, probably earning more than all of us.\n\nSo, what's the moral of the story? Even if you're a chicken, if you're brave enough to cross roads, you can end up working in Silicon Valley!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nSc