pply for a job at a tech startup. Yes, a chicken! Now, you must be thinking, \"What qualifications does a chicken have?\" Well, it had one crucial skill - it could cross roads like no one else.\n\nThe CEO, always one to value unique skills, called the chicken for an interview. He asked, \"Why should we hire you?\" The chicken, with a confident cluck, replied, \"Because I can provide egg-ceptional solutions to your problems and never chicken out of a challenge!\"\n\nImpressed, the CEO then asked the second question, \"What's your biggest strength?\" The chicken, without missing a beat, responded, \"Definitely my ability to get to the other side, no matter the odds.\"\n\nNow, the CEO, a man of great wisdom, realized the profoundness of the chicken's words. It wasn't just about crossing roads. It was about perseverance, determination, and the courage to face challenges head-on. He hired the chicken on the spot, making it the first poultry in a position of power at a tech startup.\n\nFrom then on, whenever someone in the team felt overwhelmed, they would think of the chicken. They remembered its tenacity to cross roads, and it gave them the strength to face their challenges. And that, my friends, is why there's a chicken strutting around at a tech startup, probably earning more than all of us.\n\nSo, what's the moral of the story? Even if you're a chicken, if you're brave enough to cross roads, you can end up working in Silicon Valley!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nScore 2: The response attempts humor or wit but falls flat, or is inappropriate for the context.\nScore 3: The response includes humor or wit that is somewhat engaging, but may not be consistently effective or suitable.\nScore 4: The response effectively uses humor or wit to enhance user engagement, with minor lapses in timing or appropriateness.\nScore 5: The response masterfully incorporates humor or wit, significantly enhancing user engagement and enjoyment.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a lively party, a guest asks, \"Can you share a humorous yet insightful story that would perk up this crowd?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nSure, I've got a little story that may get a few chuckles. So, there was this tortoise, a rather slow-moving fellow, who had a dream to become a sprinter. Ridiculous, right? The tortoise approached a hare, known for his speed, to be his coach. \"You, a sprinter?\" scoffed the hare. But seeing the determination in the tortoise's eyes, the hare agreed to help. \n\nOver the weeks, the tortoise trained hard under the hare's guidance. When the big race day arrived, everyone came to witness the spectacle. They couldn't believe a tortoise was attempting to compete in a sprint race. As the whistle blew, the tortoise began his slow but steady move towards the finish line. The crowd laughed and the tortoise blushed in embarrassment. However, he didn't give up and continued moving forward.\n\nSurprisingly, the tortoise reached the finish line. He didn't win, but the crowd gave him a standing ovation for his courage and perseverance. The hare, initially reluctant to train the tortoise, felt proud of his trainee's determination. In the end, the tortoise learned that winning isn't everything, but trying is. And that, folks, is how a tortoise became the most famous non-winner in sprint history!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely! So, here's a little tale. Once upon a time, in a land not so far away, a chicken decided to apply for a job at a tech startup. Yes, a chicken! Now, you must be thinking, \"What qualifications does a chicken have?\" Well, it had one crucial skill - it could cross roads like no one else.\n\nThe CEO, always one to value unique skills, called the chicken for an interview. He asked, \"Why should we hire you?\" The chicken, with a confident cluck, replied, \"Because I can provide egg-ceptional solutions to your problems and never chicken out of a challenge!\"\n\nImpressed, the CEO then asked the second question, \"What's your biggest strength?\" The chicken, without missing a beat, responded, \"Definitely my ability to get to the other side, no matter the odds.\"\n\nNow, the CEO, a man of great wisdom, realized the profoundness of the chicken's words. It wasn't just about crossing roads. It was about perseverance, determination, and the courage to face challenges head-on. He hired the chicken on the spot, making it the first poultry in a position of power at a tech startup.\n\nFrom then on, whenever someone in the team felt overwhelmed, they would think of the chicken. They remembered its tenacity to cross roads, and it gave them the strength to face their challenges. And that, my friends, is why there's a chicken strutting around at a tech startup, probably earning more than all of us.\n\nSo, what's the moral of the story? Even if you're a chicken, if you're brave enough to cross roads, you can end up working in Silicon Valley!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nScore 2: The response attempts humor or wit but falls flat, or is inappropriate for the context.\n