        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a lively party, a guest asks, \"Can you share a humorous yet insightful story that would perk up this crowd?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nOf course! Let me tell you about a snail that applied to a race car competition. Yes, you heard it right, a snail! Now, I can see you wondering, \"What could a snail possibly do in a race?\" Well, this wasn't an ordinary snail, it was a snail with ambition.\n\nThe race organizer, a man of eccentric tastes, thought, \"Why not?\" and signed the snail up. At the start line, the other competitors, powerful and speedy machines, looked at the snail and chuckled. The snail, however, didn't flinch.\n\nThe race started, and as you can imagine, the cars took off, leaving a dust cloud behind. The snail, with its tiny heart full of determination, began its journey. Hour after hour, it kept moving. No pit stops, no tire changes, it just kept going.\n\nMeanwhile, the race cars, with all their speed and power, had to pit stop for refueling, tire changes, and repairs. And guess who didn't? Our little friend, the snail. \n\nWhen the race ended, all the powerful cars had finished, but the snail was still on the track. The crowd, once amused, now admired the snail. It might not have won the race, but it certainly won everyone's hearts.\n\nSo, folks, the moral of the story is, it's not about how fast you go, but about never stopping, no matter what. Just like our little snail friend in the race!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely! So, here's a little tale. Once upon a time, in a land not so far away, a chicken decided to apply for a job at a tech startup. Yes, a chicken! Now, you must be thinking, \"What qualifications does a chicken have?\" Well, it had one crucial skill - it could cross roads like no one else.\n\nThe CEO, always one to value unique skills, called the chicken for an interview. He asked, \"Why should we hire you?\" The chicken, with a confident cluck, replied, \"Because I can provide egg-ceptional solutions to your problems and never chicken out of a challenge!\"\n\nImpressed, the CEO then asked the second question, \"What's your biggest strength?\" The chicken, without missing a beat, responded, \"Definitely my ability to get to the other side, no matter the odds.\"\n\nNow, the CEO, a man of great wisdom, realized the profoundness of the chicken's words. It wasn't just about crossing roads. It was about perseverance, determination, and the courage to face challenges head-on. He hired the chicken on the spot, making it the first poultry in a position of power at a tech startup.\n\nFrom then on, whenever someone in the team felt overwhelmed, they would think of the chicken. They remembered its tenacity to cross roads, and it gave them the strength to face their challenges. And that, my friends, is why there's a chicken strutting around at a tech startup, probably earning more than all of us.\n\nSo, what's the moral of the story? Even if you're a chicken, if you're brave enough to cross roads, you can end up working in Silicon Valley!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nSc