        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a lively party, a guest asks, \"Can you share a humorous yet insightful story that would perk up this crowd?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nSure, I can do that! So, there was this ant who wanted to move to a new place. Yes, an ant! Imagine that, right? The ant just packed up its belongings in a tiny ant suitcase and off it went. It didn't have a clear plan in mind, it just wanted something different.\n\nNow, this ant had no idea about the big, dangerous world out there. As it walked and walked, it came across a big, scary spider. The spider asked, \"Where are you going?\" The ant replied, \"I'm moving to a new place.\"\n\nThe spider laughed, \"You? Moving? You're just an ant.\" The ant, with all its might, said, \"Yes, I am an ant, but I have a dream.\" The spider couldn't help but laugh, \"A dream? What can an ant dream about?\"\n\nUndeterred, the ant continued its journey, crossing hills of sand, avoiding streams of water, and climbing over twigs and leaves. The ant was not afraid. It had a dream and it was going to fulfill it.\n\nSo, the moral of the story is that everyone has dreams. It doesn't matter how big or small you are, you should always follow your dreams. Just like that little ant!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolut