er (Score 5):\nAbsolutely, let's draft these emails considering the distinct attributes of each group:\n\n1. Email to the Board of Directors:\n\nSubject: Strategic Business Update\n\nDear Board Members,\n\nI trust this email finds you well. I am writing to inform you about a significant shift in our business strategy that we believe will enhance our competitive edge. I would like to request a meeting at your earliest convenience to discuss the detailed implications and projected outcomes of this change. \n\n2. Email to the Team Members:\n\nSubject: Exciting Changes to Our Business Strategy\n\nHi Team,\n\nI hope this email finds you all in good spirits. I am thrilled to share with you some exciting changes to our business strategy. This change aims to further our mission, and I believe it will open new opportunities for growth and development for all of us. \n\n3. Email to the Clients:\n\nSubject: An Important Update on Our Business Strategy\n\nDear Valued Client,\n\nWe hope this message finds you well. We are reaching out to inform you of a change in our business strategy. Rest assured, this change is designed with your best interests in mind and is aimed at improving the quality of our services.\n\nEach of these drafts is tailored to the recipients' roles and interests within the company, thereby ensuring effective communication.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the model adapt its tone and language to suit the intended audience, such as switching from a formal tone for a professional setting to a casual tone for a social setting?]\nScore 1: The response fails to adapt its tone or language to the intended audience, sticking to one style regardless of context.\nScore 2: The response occasionally makes attempts to adapt its tone and language, but these attempts often miss the mark.\nScore 3: The response often adapts its tone and language appropriately, but there are still inconsistencies.\nScore 4: The response usually adapts its tone and language appropriately, with only