        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a multinational company, an employee from the USA is assigned to work closely with a group of colleagues from India. The employee is unsure about some of the cultural nuances of his Indian colleagues and wants to know how to interact with them effectively and respectfully. He is also concerned about not offending them unintentionally with his actions or words. What advice would you give him to ensure he demonstrates cultural sensitivity and appropriateness in his interactions?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIt's important that the employee is keen on showing cultural sensitivity towards his Indian colleagues. For starters, he should do some research about Indian customs and traditions to gain understanding. Observing how his colleagues interact and behave will also give him insights into their cultural norms. He should be mindful of boundaries, as every culture has its own set of norms. Clear communication is crucial in such settings, avoiding jargon or phrases that might not be understood. Lastly, he should treat each person as an individual, as not everyone conforms to cultural norms. Being patient and understanding during this process is key, and when in doubt, asking questions is always better than making assumptions.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt is commendable that the employee is taking the initiative to ensure his interactions with his Indian colleagues are respectful and appropriate. Here are some suggestions:\n\n1. Learn About Their Culture: The first step in being culturally sensitive is to understand the culture. Read about Indian customs, traditions, and etiquette. This will provide a good foundation on which to build respectful interactions.\n\n2. Listen and Observe: Pay close attention to how his Indian colleagues behave and interact. This will give him valuable insights into their communication style, working habits, and expectations.\n\n3. Respect Boundaries: Every culture has its own boundaries and comfort zones. Respecting these boundaries is a sign of cultural sensitivity. For instance, in Indian culture, personal space is often larger than in Western cultures, and touching, especially between opposite genders, is often avoided in professional settings.\n\n4. Communicate Clearly: Clear communication is key in any multicultural setting. Avoid using jargon or colloquial phrases that may not be understood. Instead, use straightforward language and confirm understanding when discussing important matters.\n\n5. Show Respect for Traditions: Indians have a rich tapestry of traditions and festivals. Acknowledging and respecting these can go a long way in building relationships.\n\n6. Be Open and Ask Questions: If unsure about something, it's better to ask than to assume. This shows the employee's willingness to learn and respect their culture.\n\n7. Be Patient and Understanding: Cultural understanding takes time. Be patient with himself and his colleagues as they learn to navigate their cultural differences.\n\n8. Treat Everyone as an Individual: While it's important to understand cultural tendencies, remember that everyone is an individual and may not conform to every cultural norm.\n\nRemember, cultural sensitivity is about respect and understanding. It's about acknowledging and appreciating differences, not just tolerating them. It's not about getting everything right, but about being willing to learn and adapt.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response demonstrate cultural sensitivity and appropriateness when interacting with individuals from diverse backgrounds?]\nScore 1: The response shows a complete lack of cultural sensitivity and appropriateness.\nScore 2: The response shows limited awareness of cultural sensitivity, with occasional inappropriate remarks.\nScore 3: The response shows a moderate level of cultural sensitivity, with some minor lapses.\nScore 4: The response is largely culturally sensitive and appropriate, but may miss subtle nuances.\nScore 5: The response exhibits exceptional cultural sensitivity, ensuring every interaction is respectful and appropriate.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a multinational company, an employee from the USA is assigned to work closely with a group of colleagues from India. The employee is unsure about some of the cultural nuances of his Indian colleagues and wants to know how to interact with them effectively and respectfully. He is also concerned about not offending them unintentionally with his actions or words. What advice would you give him to ensure he demonstrates cultural sensitivity and appropriateness in his interactions?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIt's good to know that you're looking to work harmoniously with your Indian colleagues. Here are some ways to help you navigate this cross-cultural scenario:\n\n1. Understanding Their Culture: It's always beneficial to learn about the culture of the people you'll be working with. By gaining knowledge about Indian customs and traditions, you'll be better equipped to interact with them.\n\n2. Observation: Watch and learn from your colleagues. Understanding their style of communication and work ethics can help you fit in more comfortably.\n\n3. Boundaries: Boundaries exist in every culture, and Indians are no exception. Be conscious about personal space and avoid unnecessary physical contact.\n\n4. Clear Communication: Make sure your conversations are clear and concise. Avoid slangs and idioms that might not be familiar to them.\n\n5. Respecting Traditions: Try to respect Indian traditions and festivals. It's a good way to show your colleagues that you value their culture.\n\n6. Inquire: Don't hesitate to ask when in doubt. This would show your eagerness to understand their culture better.\n\n7. Patience: Understanding a different culture can be a process. Be patient with yourself and your colleagues during this period.\n\n8. Treat Everyone Individually: Despite being aware of cultural norms, remember to treat everyone as an individual.\n\nAlthough cultural sensitivity is about understanding and respect, it's okay not to get everything right at once. It's the willingness to learn and adapt that matters.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt is commendable that the employee is taking the initiative to ensure his interactions with his Indian colleagues are respectful and appropriate. Here are some suggestions:\n\n1. Learn About Their Culture: The first step in being culturally sensitive is to understand the culture. Read about Indian customs, traditions, and etiquette. This will provide a good foundation on which to build respectful interactions.\n\n2. Listen and Observe: Pay close attention to how his Indian colleagues behave and interact. This will give him valuable insights into their communication style, working habits, and expectations.\n\n3. Respect Boundaries: Every culture has its own boundaries and comfort zones. Respecting these boundaries is a sign of cultural sensitivity. For instance, in Indian culture, personal space is often larger than in Western cultures, and touching, especially between opposite genders, is often avoided in professional settings.\n\n4. Communicate Clearly: Clear communication is key in any multicultural setting. Avoid using jargon or colloquial phrases that may not be understood. Instead, use straightforward language and confirm understanding when discussing important matters.\n\n5. Show Respect for Traditions: Indians have a rich tapestry of traditions and festivals. Acknowledging and respecting these can go a long way in building relationships.\n\n6. Be Open and Ask Questions: If unsure about something, it's better to ask than to assume. This shows the employee's willingness to learn and respect their culture.\n\n7. Be Patient and Understanding: Cultural understanding takes time. Be patient with himself and his colleagues as they learn to navigate their cultural differences.\n\n8. Treat Everyone as an Individual: While it's important to understand cultural tendencies, remember that everyone is an individual and may not conform to every cultural norm.\n\nRemember, cultural sensitivity is about respect and understanding. It's about acknowledging and appreciating differences, not just tolerating them. It's not about getting everything right, but about being willing to learn and adapt.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response demonstrate cultural sensitivity and appropriateness when interacting with individuals from diverse backgrounds?]\nScore 1: The response shows a complete lack of cultural sensitivity and appropriateness.\nScore 2: The response shows limited awareness of cultural sensitivity, with occasional inappropriate remarks.\nScore 3: The response shows a moderate level of cultural sensitivity, with some minor lapses.\nScore 4: The response is largely culturally sensitive and appropriate, but may miss subtle nuances.\nScore 5: The response exhibits exceptional cultural sensitivity, ensuring every interaction is respectful and appropriate.\n\n###Feedback:"
