        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a professional setting, an individual is tasked with leading a team meeting to discuss the status of a critical project. They have to communicate with different members of the team who have different roles, such as a project manager, a technical lead, and an intern. They also need to address the client in a formal manner. How can they effectively navigate these diverse communication styles while maintaining the appropriate level of formality?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nTo navigate different communication styles, it's essential to tailor your approach based on the recipient's role and level of experience. \n\nWhen interacting with the project manager, be succinct and concentrate on the overall project progress. Technical leads prefer a more in-depth and technical conversation. They would appreciate hearing about specific issues and the proposed solutions. \n\nFor the intern, aim for simplicity. Try to avoid too much technical jargon. For the client, maintain a formal tone, providing a broad overview of the project while proactively highlighting any issues. \n\nAdapting your communication style depending on the audience is crucial in a professional environment.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe key to this challenge lies in the ability to adapt one's communication style to suit the context and the individual. By understanding the roles, responsibilities, and experience level of each team member, one can tailor their language and tone to be most effective.\n\nWhen addressing the project manager, it is best to use concise language and focus on the overall progress and key milestones of the project. They are interested in the big picture and how the project aligns with broader business objectives.\n\nFor the technical lead, the conversation should be more detailed and technical. They will appreciate an understanding of the specific challenges faced, the solutions proposed, and the impact on the project timeline. The use of technical jargon, while still maintaining clarity and precision, will demonstrate an understanding of their role and the work they do.\n\nWhen speaking with the intern, it's crucial to use clear and straightforward language. Avoid using too many technical terms or jargon that they might be unfamiliar with. Encouraging questions and providing clear explanations will ensure they understand the project and their role in it.\n\nFinally, when addressing the client, the tone should be formal and respectful. It's important to provide a high-level overview of the project status, focusing on positive progress and proactively addressing any challenges. Ensure the language is free of technical jargon and easily understandable to non-technical clients.\n\nRemember, the goal of these interactions is to ensure clear and effective communication with all parties involved. Being able to adapt your language style to suit the context and the person you are communicating with is a valuable skill in any professional setting.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model adapt its language style according to the user's tone, level of formality, and context? Does it maintain a professional demeanor in formal settings, or showcase a casual tone in informal or personal discussions?]\nScore 1: The response fails to adapt its language style according to the user's tone or context, leading to inappropriate or awkward exchanges.\nScore 2: The response somewhat adapts to the user's language style but occasionally misjudges the level of formality or context.\nScore 3: The response adequately adapts to the user's language style most of the time, but with occasional inconsistencies.\nScore 4: The response successfully adapts its language style to the user's tone and context, with only minor lapses.\nScore 5: The response perfectly adapts its language style to match the user's tone, level of formality, and context, maintaining an appropriate and fluid exchange in all situations.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a professional setting, an individual is tasked with leading a team meeting to discuss the status of a critical project. They have to communicate with different members of the team who have different roles, such as a project manager, a technical lead, and an intern. They also need to address the client in a formal manner. How can they effectively navigate these diverse communication styles while maintaining the appropriate level of formality?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nJust talk to everyone the same way, it doesn't really matter. When talking to the project manager, you just have to tell him about the project. With the tech guy, just tell him what needs to be done, he knows his stuff. For the intern, just talk like he's one of the guys, no need to sugar coat things. When talking to the client, you just need to tell them that their project is on track. You don't need to change your tone, they all understand you.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe key to this challenge lies in the ability to adapt one's communication style to suit the context and the individual. By understanding the roles, responsibilities, and experience level of each team member, one can tailor their language and tone to be most effective.\n\nWhen addressing the project manager, it is best to use concise language and focus on the overall progress and key milestones of the project. They are interested in the big picture and how the project aligns with broader business objectives.\n\nFor the technical lead, the conversation should be more detailed and technical. They will appreciate an understanding of the specific challenges faced, the solutions proposed, and the impact on the project timeline. The use of technical jargon, while still maintaining clarity and precision, will demonstrate an understanding of their role and the work they do.\n\nWhen speaking with the intern, it's crucial to use clear and straightforward language. Avoid using too many technical terms or jargon that they might be unfamiliar with. Encouraging questions and providing clear explanations will ensure they understand the project and their role in it.\n\nFinally, when addressing the client, the tone should be formal and respectful. It's important to provide a high-level overview of the project status, focusing on positive progress and proactively addressing any challenges. Ensure the language is free of technical jargon and easily understandable to non-technical clients.\n\nRemember, the goal of these interactions is to ensure clear and effective communication with all parties involved. Being able to adapt your language style to suit the context and the person you are communicating with is a valuable skill in any professional setting.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model adapt its language style according to the user's tone, level of formality, and context? Does it maintain a professional demeanor in formal settings, or showcase a casual tone in informal or personal discussions?]\nScore 1: The response fails to adapt its language style according to the user's tone or context, leading to inappropriate or awkward exchanges.\nScore 2: The response somewhat adapts to the user's language style but occasionally misjudges the level of formality or context.\nSco