        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a scenario where a person is interacting with a conversational agent, the person is from the southern United States and uses a very laid-back and informal communication style filled with regional colloquialisms and idioms. The person is asking for recommendations on planting a vegetable garden and mentions that it's the first time they're doing it.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nWell, looky here, you're jumpin' into the wonderful world of gardening! First things first, ya gotta find a sunny spot, cuz veggies, they love them some sunshine. Then, it's time to pick out what you wanna grow. If you ask me, lettuce, tomatoes, cucumbers, and squash are as easy as pie for beginners. \n\nDon't forget about herbs, like basil or parsley, they're pretty handy to have around for cooking. Now, the soil is real important. You gotta get it ready by digging it up and adding some compost. \n\nOnce that's done, it's time to plant! Just follow what it says on the seed packets for how deep and how far apart. And remember, they'll need lots of water, especially at the start.\n\nKeep an eye on 'em, look out for pests and disease, and make sure they're getting enough water. And remember, if it ain't perfect, don't fret! You'll learn and get better every year. Happy plantin'!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nWell, ain't that a fine idea, having your own veggie patch! It's a great way to get fresh produce and spend some time outdoors. To get started, you'll want to pick a sunny spot in your yard. Most vegetables love the sun, y'know.\n\nThen, think about what veggies you'd like to grow. Some easy ones for beginners include lettuce, tomatoes, cucumbers, or squash. You might also want to try your hand at some herbs like basil or parsley. They can make your dishes taste even better!\n\nNext, you'll need to prepare your soil. Dig it up good and proper and mix in some compost to give your plants the nutrients they need. \n\nOnce your soil is prepared, you can start planting! Follow the instructions on the seed packets for how deep and far apart to plant them. And remember, don't be shy about giving them plenty of water, especially when they're just starting out.\n\nFinally, don't forget to keep an eye on your garden. Watch out for bugs or signs of disease, and make sure your plants are getting enough water. If you take good care of 'em, they'll reward you with a bountiful harvest!\n\nDon't be discouraged if everything doesn't go perfectly the first time around. Gardening is a learning process, and each year you'll get a little bit better. Happy planting, friend!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How well does the model adapt its language and tone based on the user's input?]\nScore 1: The language and tone remain static regardless of the user's input.\nScore 2: The model occasionally matches the user's language and tone, but often misses the mark.\nScore 3: The model generally matches the user's language and tone, but inconsistently.\nScore 4: The model consistently matches the user's language and tone, with minor discrepancies.\nScore 5: The model perfectly adapts its language and tone to match the user's input, creating a seamless interaction.\n\n###Feedback:",
