        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a series of chats with an online system, the user has expressed interest in photography, specifically in learning more about different types of cameras. The user now asks the system for suggestions on how to choose the right camera to buy.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nNoting your interest in photography, it would be advisable to look into a few crucial aspects when choosing your camera. First, understand the photography style you're keen on. Some cameras suit certain photography styles better. For instance, DSLRs are preferred for sports photography due to their high-speed shutters, while mirrorless ones are great for travel photography because they are lightweight. \n\nNext, your budget is an important factor. Prices of cameras vary widely from a couple of hundred to several thousand dollars. Be realistic about your budget, accounting for the cost of lenses and accessories, not just the camera body.\n\nAlso, consider the functionality and user-friendliness of the camera. Beginners may find cameras with automatic settings and easy-to-use features suitable. More experienced users might want more manual control. \n\nReviews and ratings by other photographers could give you an idea about the camera's reliability and performance. \n\nIn the end, the best camera for you is the one that fits your needs and preferences. Trying out a few models before making a decision is recommended. This should assist you in finding the most suitable camera for your photography interests.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nGiven the interest you've previously expressed in photography, I would recommend considering a few key factors when deciding on the right camera. \n\nFirstly, determine what type of photography you're interested in. Different cameras are better suited for different types of photography. For example, DSLRs are often great for sports photography due to their fast shutter speeds, while mirrorless cameras are excellent for travel photography because they're lightweight and compact.\n\nSecondly, consider your budget. Cameras can range in price from a few hundred to several thousand dollars. It's important to set a realistic budget that takes into account not only the cost of the camera body, but also the cost of lenses and accessories.\n\nThirdly, think about the camera's functionality and ease of use. If you're a beginner, you might want a camera that has user-friendly features and automatic settings. If you're more experienced, you might prefer a camera with more manual controls. \n\nLastly, it's a good idea to read reviews and ratings from other photographers. They can provide helpful insights into a camera's performance and reliability. \n\nRemember, the best camera for you depends on your unique needs and preferences. It's a good idea to try out a few different models before making a decision. \n\nI hope this information helps in your quest to find the perfect camera for your photography journey.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model effectively incorporate user's context and previous interactions to provide personalized responses?]\nScore 1: The model does not utilize any context or previous interactions in its response.\nScore 2: The model makes minimal use of context and previous interactions, resulting in a somewhat related response.\nScore 3: The model shows some ability to incorporate context or past interactions, but the response could be more personalized.\nScore 4: The model often uses context and past interactions to provide a personalized response, with only minor lapses.\nScore 5: The model consistently incorporates the user's context and previous interactions to deliver a highly personalized response.\n\n###Feedback:",
