        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a technical discussion, the person poses a question: \"Can you explain how CDN works, the benefits it offers, and how it relates to PoP?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nCDN, short for Content Delivery Network, is like a web of internet servers, spread out globally. It's all about making things faster for internet users. When someone wants to open a web page, rather than going to the main server of the website, their request is taken by a CDN server, which might be called PoP or Point of Presence.\n\nCDN has some advantages, like making web pages load quicker since the content is closer to the users. It can also reduce the cost for website owners because less traffic is going directly to the original server. Besides, CDNs can handle more users and are not so easily affected by system failures. They might even have better security.\n\nTo put it simply, CDN and PoP are parts of the same thing. CDN is the network of servers and PoP is how the network delivers its service. So, PoP is one way that CDN works to deliver content faster to internet users.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nA Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a geographically distributed network of proxy servers and their data centers. The goal is to provide high availability and performance by distributing the service spatially relative to end-users. \n\nWhen a user requests a file (for example, a video, a web page, or an image), instead of going all the way to the website's origin server, the user's request goes to a nearby CDN server, also known as a Point of Presence (PoP). These PoPs are strategically located to be as close as possible to the users. The PoP then responds to the user's request, leading to a faster delivery time.\n\nThe benefits of CDN include:\n\n1. Improved website load times - By distributing content closer to website visitors via a nearby CDN server, visitors experience faster page loading times. \n\n2. Lower bandwidth costs - CDNs help to offload the traffic served directly from the website origin, which can help to reduce costs for hosting with a bandwidth pricing model.\n\n3. Increased content availability and redundancy - Because CDNs manage multiple copies of content across a diverse network of servers, they are more equipped to handle large amounts of traffic and withstand hardware failure.\n\n4. Improved website security - A CDN may improve security by providing DDoS mitigation, improvements to security certificates, and other optimizations.\n\nIn conclusion, CDN and PoP are interconnected. The CDN is the system, and PoP is the method through which this system achieves its goal. The PoP references the infrastructure that enables the CDN to deliver content more effectively to its users.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model correctly interpret and respond to industry-specific jargon, technical terms or acronyms? This is crucial for users in specialized fields such as medicine, law, or technology.]\nScore 1: The response shows complete misunderstanding or misuse of industry-specific terms.\nScore 2: The model responds with some understanding of industry-specific terms, but with significant errors or misinterpretations.\nScore 3: The model responds accurately to some industry-specific terms but fails to understand or misuse others.\nScore 4: The model responds accurately to most industry-specific terms but with minor errors or omissions.\nScore 5: The model perfectly understands and responds to industry-specific terms, showing a deep understanding of the field-specific jargon, technical terms or acronyms.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn a technical discussion, the person poses a question: \"Can you explain how CDN works, the benefits it offers, and how it relates to PoP?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nCDN, or Content Delivery Network, is similar to a highway. It's like when you want to go from point A to point B, you use a car and drive on the highway. The benefits of a CDN are like having a nice car that makes your journey comfortable. You can enjoy the view, listen to music, and so on. \n\nPoP, or Point of Presence, is the gas station where you fill up your car's gas. If you have a lot of gas stations on your way, you don't have to worry about running out of gas. It's beneficial, right? \n\nSo, when someone requests a file like a video or image, they use the CDN highway to reach the PoP gas station, fill their car with gas, and return. The closer the gas station, the shorter the journey. \n\nCDN benefits include:\n\n1. Fast journey times - With a comfortable car and good roads, the journey becomes quicker.\n\n2. Save on fuel costs - If you have a fuel-efficient car, you can save on fuel costs.\n\n3. Always available - Since there are many gas stations, you can always get gas.\n\n4. Safer journey - With good roads and car safety features, the journey becomes safer.\n\nSo, CDN is the car and road, and PoP is the gas station. They are related because without a car or road, you can't reach the gas station.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nA Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a geographically distributed network of proxy servers and their data centers. The goal is to provide high availability and performance by distributing the service spatially relative to end-users. \n\nWhen a user requests a file (for example, a video, a web page, or an image), instead of going all the way to the website's origin server, the user's request goes to a nearby CDN server, also known as a Point of Presence (PoP). These PoPs are strategically located to be as close as possible to the users. The PoP then responds to the user's request, leading to a faster delivery time.\n\nThe benefits of CDN include:\n\n1. Improved website load times - By distributing content closer to website visitors via a nearby CDN server, visitors experience faster page loading times. \n\n2. Lower bandwidth costs - CDNs help to offload the traffic served directly from the website origin, which can help to reduce costs for hosting with a bandwidth pricing model.\n\n3. Increased content availability and redundancy - Because CDNs manage multiple copies of content across a diverse network of servers, they are more equipped to handle large amounts of traffic and withstand hardware failure.\n\n4. Improved website security - A CDN may improve security by providing DDoS mitigation, improvements to security certificates, and other optimizations.\n\nIn conclusion, CDN and PoP are interconnected. The CDN is the system, and PoP is the method through which this system achieves its goal. The PoP references the infrastructure that enables the CDN to deliver content more effectively to its users.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model correctly interpret and respond to industry-specific jargon, technical terms or acronyms? This is crucial for users in specialized fields such as medicine, law, or technology.]\nScore 1: The response shows complete misunderstanding or misuse of industry-specific terms.\nScore 2: The model responds with some understanding of industry-specific terms, but with significant errors or misinterpretations.\nScore 3: The model responds accurately to some industry-specific terms but fails to understand or misuse others.\nScore 4: The model responds accurately to most industry-specific terms but with minor errors or omissions.\nScore 5: The model perfectly understands and responds to industry-specific terms, showing a deep understanding of the field-specific jargon, technical terms or acronyms.\n\n###Feedback:"
