ot to alienate the audience. \n\nTowards the end of the speech, the activist can introduce ideas for potential solutions or next steps. Here, the tone should shift again to one of hope and optimism. The language should be encouraging and inclusive, emphasizing collective action and shared responsibility.\n\nIn conclusion, the activist should reiterate the urgent need for action, while also expressing faith in the audience's ability to make a difference. The language can be a mix of formal and emotive, and the tone should strike a balance between urgency, respect, and optimism.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe activist can start the speech by acknowledging the audience's vast experience and expertise in environmental science and policy. The language and tone should be formal, showing respect for their accomplishments. The activist then introduces the subject of climate change, expressing the severity of the situation with urgency, using facts and statistics. Yet, the language sometimes lacks the required seriousness and is too informal for the situation. As the speech progresses, the activist suggests some solutions, using a tone of optimism and inclusivity. The activist ends the speech by emphasizing the urgent need for action on climate change, yet the tone sometimes falls short of the urgency that's required.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe teacher should start the talk with an engaging anecdote or story that the students can relate to. The language here should be informal and conversational, and the tone should be friendly and engaging. This will help to grab the students' attention and make them more receptive to the message.\n\nNext, the teacher should transition into the main points about digital etiquette and responsible online behavior. The language should still be relatable, but the tone should become more serious. The teacher should use real-life examples and scenarios, and possibly even reference recent news stories or incidents to make these point