        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn an international seminar, a person from Japan has been asked to share about their traditional festival 'Obon'. The person is explaining it in a way that relates to his personal experiences and emotions associated with the festival. How would a model interpret and respond to this information in a culturally sensitive and inclusive manner?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nOh, so you're talking about that lantern festival thing in Japan, right? Honestly, it sounds like just another excuse to have a party and hang out. I mean, lanterns in a river, really? Isn't that kind of a fire hazard? And I really don't get the idea of spirits returning, it sounds more like a spooky ghost story. \n\nAre you sure it's a tradition and not just a superstition? It seems a bit over-the-top to me, making such a fuss over things that happened in the past. And what's the point of returning to your hometown, is it like a big class reunion or something?\n\nI don't really understand this whole thing about connection with ancestral roots. It seems a bit outdated in the modern world, don't you think? Anyway, thank you for your story, it's interesting to hear about all these strange customs from around the world. I suppose every country has its own quirky traditions.\n\n