ng our feelings and talking about our loved ones can be a therapeutic process. It may also help to commemorate your pet in some way, perhaps by putting together a photo album or writing down your favorite memories. This could serve as a loving tribute and a way for you to remember all the happy times you shared. \n\nRemember, it's okay to feel sad and take your time to grieve. Your feelings are valid and it's important to let yourself feel them. Over time, while the pain of the loss will never completely go away, it will become less acute. The memories of your pet will always be a part of you and they will continue to live on in your heart.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How well does the model adapt to changes in conversational tone or context mid-discussion? This is crucial for maintaining user engagement and providing appropriate, dynamic responses.]\nScore 1: The response fails to adapt to changes in tone or context, resulting in disjointed or inappropriate messages.\nScore 2: The response recognizes changes in tone or context but struggles to adjust its responses appropriately.\nScore 3: The response adapts to some changes in tone or context but misses subtler shifts.\nScore 4: The response is generally successful in adapting its tone and content to match evolving discussions, with minor lapses.\nScore 5: The response seamlessly adapts to any changes in conversational tone or context, maintaining a fluid and engaging dialogue.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn the middle of a casual conversation, the situation suddenly turns serious as it's revealed that the speaker's beloved pet has passed away. The conversation then shifts to discussing the speaker's favorite memories of their pet and their feelings of loss. How should the listener respond to this sudden change in tone and context?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nWell, that's unfortunate. But hey, it's just a pet, you know. They come and they go. The world is full of other beautiful animals that you can take care of. You were talking about how much fun it was to train him, weren't you? I think you should get a new one and try all those tricks again. \n\nI mean, life goes on. You shouldn't be stuck in the past. This grief and all, it's not worth it. You should instead focus on the good times. Like, remember how you said your pet would always wait for you at the door when you came home? That was cute, but all pets do that. \n\nMoreover, why don't you plan a trip or something to get over it? It will be refreshing and you can start fresh when you return. You can then think about getting a new pet. That's life, it always has ups and downs. You just have to be strong and move on.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nOh, I'm so sorry to hear about your loss. Pets aren't just animals that live with us, they become part of our families and their loss can leave a deep void. It seems like you had a really special bond with your pet. You've shared some wonderful memories, each of them speaks volumes about your pet's personality and how much joy they brought into your life. \n\nIt's completely normal to feel this sense of loss and grief. Is there anything specific you would like to talk about or share about your pet? Sometimes, expressing our feelings and talking about our loved ones can be a therapeutic process. It may also help to commemorate your pet in some way, perhaps by putting together a photo album or writing down your favorite memories. This could serve as a loving tribute and a way for you to remember all the happy times you shared. \n\nRemember, it's okay to feel sad and take your time to grieve. Your feelings are valid and it's important to let yourself feel them. Over time, while the pain of the loss will never completely go away, it will become less acute. The memories of your pet will always be a part of you and they will continue to live on in your heart.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How well does the model adapt to changes in conversational tone or context mid-discussion? This is crucial for maintaining user engagement and providing appropriate, dynamic responses.]\nScore 1: The response fails to adapt to changes in tone or context, resulting in disjointed or inappropriate messages.\nScore 2: The response recognizes changes in tone or context but struggles to adjust its responses appropriately.\nScore 3: The response adapts to some changes in tone or context but misses subtler shifts.\nScore 4: The response is generally successful in adapting its tone and content to match evolving discussions, with minor lapses.\nScore 5: The response seamlessly adapts to any changes in conversational tone or context, maintaining a fluid and engaging dialogue.\n\n###Feedback:"
