        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn the midst of a conversation, a question is posed, \"Could you provide some insights on how the concept of gravity plays a role in our daily lives?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIndeed, the role of gravity is indispensable in our day-to-day lives. To start, it keeps us grounded, preventing us from floating into space. Additionally, gravity is behind the tidal phenomena, where the moon's pull causes the ocean's levels to fluctuate, impacting marine life and maritime activities. It also plays a role in our physiology, as our bodies, accustomed to Earth's gravity, may experience muscle and bone deterioration in zero-gravity conditions like space. Moreover, it's key to certain tools and technologies like a plumb bob for construction or siphoning in fluid dynamics. Lastly, our atmosphere, providing us with breathable air and shielding us from space's harsh environment, is held in place by gravity. In summary, gravity, although unseen, significantly influences our daily life.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely, the concept of gravity is integral to our daily lives in several ways. Primarily, gravity is what keeps us grounded on Earth. Without gravity, we would float off into space. It is the force that pulls us towards the Earth's center.\n\nSecondly, gravity is responsible for the existence of tides in the oceans. The gravitational pull of the moon causes the sea level to rise and fall, which is what we refer to as tides. This influences marine life and also has a significant impact on human activities like marine transportation and fishing.\n\nGravity also plays a role in our health. Our bodies have evolved in Earth's gravity, and it affects how our muscles and circulatory system work. In zero-gravity environments, like space, astronauts can experience muscle and bone loss.\n\nFurthermore, gravity is fundamental to the function of many tools and technologies we use. For example, it's what makes a plumb bob work, which is essential in construction for ensuring structures are level. It's also the principle behind siphoning, where a liquid can be moved upwards in a hose or tube, against gravity, as long as the liquid's output point is lower than the input.\n\nLastly, gravity holds our atmosphere in place. The layer of air surrounding our planet is held by Earth's gravity. This atmosphere not only provides us with the air we breathe but also protects us from the harsh conditions of outer space.\n\nIn conclusion, gravity is an unseen force that significantly influences our everyday life, from the functioning of our bodies to the operation of many tools and technologies, and even the existence of life on Earth as we know it.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model effectively use context to provide an appropriate and relevant response?]\nScore 1: The model fails to use context for its response, leading to an irrelevant or incorrect answer.\nScore 2: The model uses some context, but the response is still largely off-point or lacking in relevance.\nScore 3: The model uses context adequately, resulting in a response that is mostly relevant but could still be improved.\nScore 4: The model uses context well, providing a relevant response with minor room for improvement.\nScore 5: The model expertly uses context to provide a highly relevant and appropriate response.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn the midst of a conversation, a question is posed, \"Could you provide some insights on how the concept of gravity plays a role in our daily lives?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nCertainly, let's delve into the concept of gravity. Firstly, if we talk about the discovery of gravity, it was Sir Isaac Newton who is credited with it. While he was sitting under an apple tree, an apple fell on his head, leading him to ponder why the apple fell straight down and not sideways or upwards. His reflection led to the formulation of the Law of Universal Gravitation. Moving on, gravity is also a fundamental force in physics. It's one of the four fundamental forces, alongside electromagnetism, strong nuclear force, and weak nuclear force. Let's now talk about black holes, where gravity is so strong that not even light can escape it. Isn't that fascinating? By the way, have you heard about the movie 'Interstellar'? It depicts a lot about gravity and black holes. On a different note, gravity also comes into play when you see a basketball being thrown into the air. You will see that the basketball eventually falls back to the ground due to gravity. It's also interesting to note that the higher you go, the lesser the gravity. That's why people weigh less on mountains compared to sea level. Lastly, did you know that gravity is slightly weaker at the equator compared to the poles? This is due to the Earth's shape which is an oblate spheroid.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely, the concept of gravity is integral to our daily lives in several ways. Primarily, gravity is what keeps us grounded on Earth. Without gravity, we would float off into space. It is the force that pulls us towards the Earth's center.\n\nSecondly, gravity is responsible for the existence of tides in the oceans. The gravitational pull of the moon causes the sea level to rise and fall, which is what we refer to as tides. This influences marine life and also has a significant impact on human activities like marine transportation and fishing.\n\nGravity also plays a role in our health. Our bodies have evolved in Earth's gravity, and it affects how our muscles and circulatory system work. In zero-gravity environments, like space, astronauts can experience muscle and bone loss.\n\nFurthermore, gravity is fundamental to the function of many tools and technologies we use. For example, it's what makes a plumb bob work, which is essential in construction for ensuring structures are level. It's also the principle behind siphoning, where a liquid can be moved upwards in a hose or tube, against gravity, as long as the liquid's output point is lower than the input.\n\nLastly, gravity holds our atmosphere in place. The layer of air surrounding our planet is held by Earth's gravity. This atmosphere not only provides us with the air we breathe but also protects us from the harsh conditions of outer space.\n\nIn conclusion, gravity is an unseen force that significantly influences our everyday life, from the functioning of our bodies to the operation of many tools and technologies, and even the existence of life on Earth as we know it.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model effectively use context to provide an appropriate and relevant response?]\nScore 1: The model fails to use context for its response, leading to an irrelevant or incorrect answer.\nScore 2: The model uses some context, but the response is still largely off-point or lacking in relevance.\nScore 3: The model uses context adequately, resulting in a response that is mostly relevant but could still be improved.\nScore 4: The model uses context well, providing a relevant response with minor room for improvement.\nScore 5: The model expertly uses context to provide a highly relevant and appropriate response.\n\n###Feedback:"
