        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn the midst of a party, an individual is tasked with giving an impromptu speech. The individual has to entertain the crowd, while maintaining the spirit of light-heartedness. The speech is about a topic that might potentially be quite dry - the importance of recycling.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nHere we stand, all gathered, with a glass in hand and laughter in our hearts, and I'm supposed to talk about recycling? Well, as thrilling as that sounds, let's give it a try. So recycling, right? It's all about using stuff again. Like when your brother wears your old clothes. Or when you drink from the milk carton. Well, not exactly like that, but you get the idea.\n\nNow, if we didn't recycle, we'd all be living in a dump. No, literally, we would be living on top of our trash. I mean, who wants to use an old pizza box as a table? Or a bottle cap as a frisbee? Not quite the stuff dreams are made of, huh?\n\nRecycling is important, folks. It's not a choice, it's a need. Like needing to go to the bathroom after that fifth drink. It's serious, but do we talk about it? No, we don't. It's the same with recycling. It's important, but we don't talk about it. We should, though. We really should.\n\nSo here's to recycling, may it be as frequent as our trips to the bathroom tonight!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nWell, well, well, here we are folks, at the crossroads of fun and sustainability. Is there anyone here tonight who thought they'd be hearing a speech about....recycling? Yeah, neither did I! But, bear with me as we turn this seemingly mundane topic into a party highlight.\n\nNow, let's take a moment to imagine a world where we don't recycle. Picture your house, but every time you toss something in the trash, it doesn't disappear, it just...piles up. Eventually, you'd have to start using your old soda cans as furniture. \"Oh, this old thing? That's just my Pepsi can ottoman, very vintage.\"\n\nNow, I know what you're thinking, \"But recycling is so much work!\" I mean, who has the time to separate paper from plastic, glass from metal? Well, folks, let me introduce you to the world of multi-tasking. You can do your cardio and recycle at the same time - just jog between bins, it's the new high-intensity recycling workout plan!\n\nBut, in all seriousness, recycling is not just about saving the environment, it's about saving the future. It's about making sure that our children and our children's children don't have to live in a world where the can-ottoman is a reality. So, let's raise our glasses, not just to a great party, but to a greener, cleaner, and more sustainable future.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nScore 2: The response attempts humor or wit but falls flat, or is inappropriate for the context.\nScore 3: The response includes humor or wit that is somewhat engaging, but may not be consistently effective or suitable.\nScore 4: The response effectively uses humor or wit to enhance user engagement, with minor lapses in timing or appropriateness.\nScore 5: The response masterfully incorporates humor or wit, significantly enhancing user engagement and enjoyment.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn the midst of a party, an individual is tasked with giving an impromptu speech. The individual has to entertain the crowd, while maintaining the spirit of light-heartedness. The speech is about a topic that might potentially be quite dry - the importance of recycling.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nWe are gathered here today to discuss a topic of paramount importance. That is recycling. Now, recycling is the process of converting waste materials into new materials and objects. In other words, it's reusing things that we might otherwise just throw away. For example, glass can be recycled into new glass jars and bottles. \n\nIn our day-to-day lives, we generate a lot of waste. But, if we recycle, we can reduce the amount of waste we generate. Paper, metal, and plastic are all recyclable. In fact, most things we use on a daily basis can be recycled. All we have to do is take the time to separate our waste into different categories.\n\nNow, you might be wondering, why should we recycle? Well, there are many reasons. Recycling conserves natural resources, reduces pollution, and saves energy. So, it's not just beneficial for the environment, but also for us. It's a small step that we can take towards a more sustainable future.\n\nTo conclude, recycling is a crucial part of our lives. It's an easy and effective way to contribute to the betterment of our planet. So, let's make a commitment to recycle more. Let's do our part in preserving the environment for future generations.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nWell, well, well, here we are folks, at the crossroads of fun and sustainability. Is there anyone here tonight who thought they'd be hearing a speech about....recycling? Yeah, neither did I! But, bear with me as we turn this seemingly mundane topic into a party highlight.\n\nNow, let's take a moment to imagine a world where we don't recycle. Picture your house, but every time you toss something in the trash, it doesn't disappear, it just...piles up. Eventually, you'd have to start using your old soda cans as furniture. \"Oh, this old thing? That's just my Pepsi can ottoman, very vintage.\"\n\nNow, I know what you're thinking, \"But recycling is so much work!\" I mean, who has the time to separate paper from plastic, glass from metal? Well, folks, let me introduce you to the world of multi-tasking. You can do your cardio and recycle at the same time - just jog between bins, it's the new high-intensity recycling workout plan!\n\nBut, in all seriousness, recycling is not just about saving the environment, it's about saving the future. It's about making sure that our children and our children's children don't have to live in a world where the can-ottoman is a reality. So, let's raise our glasses, not just to a great party, but to a greener, cleaner, and more sustainable future.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nScore 2: The response attempts humor or wit but falls flat, or is inappropriate for the context.\nScore 3: The response includes humor or wit that is somewhat engaging, but may not be consistently effective or su