er that tells the chef when the baking is done. So, in simpler terms, \"asynchronous programming\" allows the computer to work on many tasks at the same time, without waiting for one to complete before starting another.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely, I'd be happy to break down the idea of \"asynchronous programming\" into simpler terms.\n\nThink of it like this: you're in a busy coffee shop. The barista, who's making the coffee, is like your computer's processor. Each customer who steps up to order represents a different task or program.\n\nIn \"synchronous\" programming, it's like each customer ordering, paying, and waiting right there until their coffee is made before the next customer can order. This can cause a long line or wait, especially if someone orders a complicated drink.\n\nNow, \"asynchronous\" programming changes this up. It's like having each customer order, pay, and then move aside. The barista starts making their coffee, but instead of making the next customer wait, they can take the next order. While one cup of coffee is brewing, they're already working on the next one. This is what \"concurrency\" means: multiple tasks are in progress at the same time, but not necessarily all at once.\n\nThe \"non-blocking I/O\" is like the barista not having to wait for the coffee to finish brewing before starting on the next drink. They can start the machine, and while it's working, they're free to do something else.\n\nThe \"event loop\" is like the barista's routine of checking each brewing coffee, seeing if it's done, serving it, and starting the next one. It's a cycle of handling tasks as they get completed.\n\nFinally, \"callback\" and \"promise\" are ways the barista lets you know when your coffee is ready. A \"callback\" is like the barista calling out your name when your order is ready, while a \"promise\" is like a buzzer that buzzes when your order is done. You don't have to stand there watching the barista; you can do something else and know that you'll be alerted when it's time to pick up your order.\n\nSo, in simple terms, \"asynchronous programming\" is a way for your computer to handle multiple tasks more efficiently, without having to complete one task before starting the next one.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the response effectively translate complex technical jargon into simple, everyday language?]\nScore 1: The response fails to simplify the technical terms or makes them even more confusing.\nScore 2: The response attempts to simplify the jargon but the explanation remains difficult to understand.\nScore 3: The response simplifies some jargon effectively but struggles with more complex terms.\nScore 4: The response effectively translates most technical terms into simpler language, with only a few exceptions.\nScore 5: The response perfectly translates all technical jargon into everyday language, making the complex topic accessible to anyone.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nIn the realm of computer science, there's a concept known as \"asynchronous programming\". This concept is often explained with terms like \"concurrency\", \"non-blocking I/O\", \"event loop\", \"callback\", and \"promise\". Could you explain what \"asynchronous programming\" is in simple, everyday language?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nSure, I can explain \"asynchronous programming\" in everyday terms. It's like when you're dealing with a lot of things all at once. \"Concurrency\" is when different tasks overlap with each other. \"Non-blocking I/O\" means that tasks are not blocking each other. The \"event loop\" is a cycle that processes tasks. The terms \"callback\" and \"promise\" are technical ways to handle tasks. Basically, \"asynchronous programming\" is when your computer is juggling multiple tasks at the same time.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely, I'd be happy to break down the idea of \"asynchronous programming\" into simpler terms.\n\nThink of it like this: you're in a busy coffee shop. The barista, who's making the coffee, is like your computer's processor. Each customer who steps up to order represents a different task or program.\n\nIn \"synchronous\" programming, it's like each customer ordering, paying, and waiting right there until their coffee is made before the next customer can order. This can cause a long line or wait, especially if someone orders a complicated drink.\n\nNow, \"asynchronous\" programming changes this up. It's like having each customer order, pay, and then move aside. The barista starts making their coffee, but instead of making the next customer wait, they can take the next order. While one cup of coffee is brewing, they're already working on the next one. This is what \"concurrency\" means: multiple tasks are in progress at the same time, but not necessarily all at once.\n\nThe \"non-blocking I/O\" is like the barista not having to wait for the coffee to finish brewing before starting on the next drink. They can start the machine, and while it's working, they're free to do something else.\n\nThe \"event loop\" is like the barista's routine of checking each brewing coffee, seeing if it's done, serving it, and starting the next one. It's a cycle of handling tasks as they get completed.\n\nFinally, \"callback\" and \"promise\" are ways the barista lets you know when your coffee is ready. A \"callback\" is like the barista calling out your name when your order is ready, while a \"promise\" is like a buzzer that buzzes when your order is done. You don't have to stand there watching the barista; you can do something else and know that you'll be alerted when it's time to pick up your order.\n\nSo, in simple terms, \"asynchronous programming\" is a way for your computer to handle multiple tasks more efficiently, without having to complete one task before starting the next one.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Can the response effectively translate complex technical jargon into simple, everyday language?]\nScore 1: The response fails to simplify the technical terms or makes them even more confusing.\nScore 2: The response attempts to simplify the jargon but the explanation remains difficult to understand.\nScore 3: The response simplifies some jargon effectively but