        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nStruggling with a new software program, a beginner level user seeks assistance to understand and navigate the software. The user is not tech-savvy and is feeling frustrated and overwhelmed by the complexity of the program. The user needs clear, simple instructions, with a patient and empathetic approach to their emotional state.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe software program you're dealing with might seem tricky at first, but don't worry, we'll sort it out. First, notice the menus at the top. They have different categories like 'File', 'Edit', 'View', and more. Familiarize yourself with these menus and understand their functions. \n\nSecondly, the largest part of the screen is the main work area where most of your tasks will be carried out. It could be a blank page, a grid, a 3D space, depending on the software. You will see many buttons and options there, learn about them gradually. \n\nOn the sides, you'll find panels or toolbars with shortcuts to commonly used tools or features. Take time to comprehend their functionality. \n\nTo save your work in progress, find the 'Save' or 'Save As...' option in the 'File' menu. \n\nRemember, it's okay to feel daunted, everyone starts from somewhere. Take your time, take breaks and don't shy away from asking for help. You're doing a good job. \n\nIf you need more clarification or have any questions, feel free to ask. We'll navigate this together.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAbsolutely, it's understandable that new software can seem daunting at first, but rest assured, I'm here to guide you through it step by step. Let's take it slow and tackle this together.\n\nFirstly, let's focus on the basic layout of the software. Generally, all software has a menu bar at the top, this is where you'll find most of the features you'll need. It's usually split into categories like 'File', 'Edit', 'View', etc. Spend some time familiarizing yourself with these menus, you don't need to remember everything, just get a general sense of where things are.\n\nNext, let's talk about the main work area. This is usually the largest part of the screen, where you'll do most of your work. Depending on the software, it could be a blank page, a grid, a 3D space, etc. Don't worry about all the buttons and options for now, we'll get to them as we need them.\n\nOn the sides, you may find additional panels or toolbars. These usually contain shortcuts to commonly used tools or features. Again, don't worry about understanding all of them right now, just know that they are there.\n\nNow, let's talk about saving your work. Most software allows you to save your work in progress so you can come back to it later. Look for the 'Save' or 'Save As...' option in the 'File' menu.\n\nRemember, it's okay to feel overwhelmed. Every expert was once a beginner. Take breaks when you need to, and don't hesitate to ask for help. You're doing great!\n\nI hope this helps you get started. If you have any specific questions or if something is unclear, feel free to ask. We'll take it one step at a time.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the language model adapt its communication style based on the user's needs, such as technical level, language proficiency, and emotional state?]\nScore 1: The language model makes no a